media: h713-hdmirx: hold the slot source for the driver's lifetime

The slot source - the display driver's flip notifier - is taken once at
the end of probe and released in remove, no longer per V4L2 stream.

A registered flip notifier is what keeps the display's vsync interrupt
enabled (AFBD +0xc4 bit 0) while no plane follows the ring:
h713_afbd_flip_users counts it and h713_afbd_vsync_irq_update() ORs it
into the bit, and reading that code proves the refcount is right as it
stands (0093a). What was missing was a holder. h713-tv does not stream, it
drives the passthrough plane, so in the "no signal" state no notifier was
registered, the plane held no vblank reference and DRM's vblank-off timer
cleared the bit: HY300 Pro bridge session 18.09., logs 12-14 - 0x056000c4
= 0 with h713-tv running, set by hand it ticks at 60 Hz (GIC 142: 636 ->
756 in two seconds), a restart of h713-tv ends with it cleared again; HY310
comparison 19.09., 01-idle - the same 0 on the board that works. With the
notifier held from probe the bit is set for as long as this driver is
bound, on both boards, and the display's vsync handler samples the ring
pointers on every frame from the first one the capture writes.

start_streaming() keeps resetting the bookkeeping, stop_streaming() keeps
completing the buffers; an event that arrives while nobody streams costs
one spinlock and a counter in h713_hdmirx_slot_event(). Because events can
now race with a stop_streaming() on another CPU, the handler completes its
buffer under slot_lock, the way stop_streaming() already does: a buffer
taken out of slot_buf[] but not yet completed would otherwise be one vb2
finds still owned by the driver after stop_streaming() returned.
vb2_buffer_done() only takes the queue's own done_lock, and that nesting -
slot_lock, then done_lock - is the one stop_streaming() established.

The start_streaming() error path that returned the buffers when the source
could not be started goes with it: nothing in there can fail any more.

Nothing keyed on a board or a firmware build. Issue #1, analysis A12 D.

Follow-up to 0136c, the last patch to shape this probe, which stays
byte-identical.

